Nonsense is exactly right. 

Maybe in some future time, we could have a scenario where supply outstrips demand (hard to imagine based on currently trajectories but anything is in theory possible), but "at the moment" we absolutely do not have more compute capacity than we have demand. 

Clearly a lot of compute capacity has come online and even more will come in coming years. Meanwhile, compute costs are also surging and have been surging all year with on-demand GPU rental rates hitting ATHs. Econ 101: when price and quantity are both increasing, it means demand increases (demand curve has shifted outward such that there's more demand at every price)!

